<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1912 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Bande Utkala Janani</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ</bdi> / <bdi lang="or-Latn">Bandē Utkaḷa Jananī</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song of 1912 in praise of &quot;Mother Utkala&quot;, the land of the Odia people. Since 2020 it is the anthem of the Indian state of Odisha.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Laxmikanta Mohapatra</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (first recorded by Gokul Mohanty in 1932; the modern notation by Balakrushna Dash)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Odia people; the Utkal Sammilani and the movement for a separate Odisha</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Odia</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Odisha, India</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>written 1912; first sung at Balasore in 1912; state anthem of Odisha from 7 June 2020</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Laxmikanta Mohapatra, written in 1912)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bande-utkala-janani">Bande Utkala Janani</a>&quot; (&quot;Glory to the Mother Utkala!&quot;) is a patriotic poem in Odia written by Laxmikanta Mohapatra in 1912. Over the centuries outsiders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and the British, had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and its boundaries had changed with each of them. In the early 20th century a movement for a separate province gathered strength, led by the Utkal Sammilani, a federation of Odias. It influenced the young Mohapatra, who wrote this and other fiery songs with a patriotic aim. The song was first sung at the Utkal Sammilani's conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasore">Balasore</a> in 1912, and the Sammilani used it as a weapon in its campaign. The poet Banchhanidhi Mohanty, who was also a good singer, sang Mohapatra's patriotic songs throughout Odisha, and at a meeting at Bhadrak where Mohapatra was honoured, the young leader Harekrushna Mahtab said that the poet's songs had inspired him a great deal. On 1 April 1936 Odisha became the first state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> to be formed on a linguistic basis.</p><p>The music was first recorded by Gokul Mohanty in 1932, and the Odissi musician Balakrushna Dash arranged the modern notation, which was later adopted for the anthem. In the early 1990s the Chief Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, Biju Patnaik, asked the Speaker of the Odisha Legislative Assembly, Yudhistir Das, to have the song played at the end of Assembly sessions, and in 1994 an all-party meeting chaired by Das adopted it to be played and sung at the valedictory session of the Assembly.</p><p>In 2020 the Chief Minister, Naveen Patnaik, encouraged people to recite &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bande-utkala-janani">Bande Utkala Janani</a>&quot; to raise the morale of the health workers fighting the coronavirus outbreak. On 7 June 2020 a meeting of the state cabinet that he chaired gave the poem the status of the anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A hymn of praise to Utkala, the Odia homeland, addressed as a mother. It pictures a land that keeps its self-respect and dignity from confidence and strength rather than from fear, and calls on its people to honour her. The mood is devoted and proud.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Odia</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Odia</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bande utkaḷa jananī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you, Mother Utkala,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଚାରୁ ହାସମୟୀ ଚାରୁ ଭାଷମୟୀ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">cāru hāsamaẏī cāru bhāṣamaẏī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of lovely smile, of lovely speech,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">jananī, jananī, jananī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, Mother, Mother,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bande utkaḷa jananī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you, Mother Utkala.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ପୂତ-ପୟୋଧି-ବିଧୌତ-ଶରୀର,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">pūta-paẏodhi-bidhauta-śarīra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your body washed by the holy sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ତାଳତମାଳ-ସୁଶୋଭିତ-ତୀର,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">tāḷatamāḷa-suśobhita-tīra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your shores made lovely by palmyra and tamāla trees,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଶୁଭ୍ରତଟିନୀକୂଳ-ଶୀକର-ସମୀର,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">śubhrataṭinīkūḷa-śīkara-samīra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your breeze bearing spray from the banks of bright rivers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">jananī, jananī, jananī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, Mother, Mother,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bande utkaḷa jananī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you, Mother Utkala.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଘନ ଘନ ବନଭୂମି ରାଜିତଅଙ୍ଗେ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">ghana ghana banabhūmi rājitaaṅge,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your limbs made splendid by dense, dense forests,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ନୀଳ ଭୂଧରମାଳା ସାଜେ ତରଙ୍ଗେ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">nīḷa bhūdharamāḷā sāje taraṅge,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>rows of blue mountains adorning you in waves,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">କଳ କଳ ମୁଖରିତ ଚାରୁ ବିହଙ୍ଗେ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">kaḷa kaḷa mukharita cāru bihaṅge,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>loud with the warbling of lovely birds,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">jananī, jananī, jananī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, Mother, Mother,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bande utkaḷa jananī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you, Mother Utkala.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ସୁନ୍ଦରଶାଳି-ସୁଶୋଭିତ-କ୍ଷେତ୍ର,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">sundaraśāḷi-suśobhita-kṣetra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your fields made lovely by fine rice,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଜ୍ଞାନବିଜ୍ଞାନ-ପ୍ରଦର୍ଶିତ-ନେତ୍ର,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">jñānabijñāna-pradarśita-netra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your eyes showing knowledge and learning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଯୋଗୀ ଋଷିଗଣ-ଊଟଜ-ପବିତ୍ର,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">yogī r̥ṣigaṇa-ūṭaja-pabitra,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>made holy by the huts of yogis and hosts of sages,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">jananī, jananī, jananī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, Mother, Mother,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bande utkaḷa jananī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you, Mother Utkala.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ସୁନ୍ଦର ମନ୍ଦିର ମଣ୍ଡିତ-ଦେଶା,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">sundara mandira maṇḍita-deśā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a land adorned with beautiful temples,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଚାରୁକଳାବଳି-ଶୋଭିତ-ବେଶା,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">cārukaḷābaḷi-śobhita-beśā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your dress made splendid by the fine arts in their rows,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ପୁଣ୍ୟ ତୀର୍ଥଚୟା-ପୂର୍ଣ୍ଣ-ପ୍ରଦେଶା</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">puṇya tīrthacaẏā-pūrṇṇa-pradeśā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your regions full of holy places of pilgrimage,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">jananī, jananī, jananī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, Mother, Mother,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bande utkaḷa jananī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you, Mother Utkala.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଉତ୍କଳ ସୁରବର-ଦର୍ପିତ-ଗେହା,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">utkaḷa surabara-darpita-gehā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Utkala, a home made proud by the best of gods,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଅରିକୁଳ-ଶୋଣିତ-ଚର୍ଚ୍ଚିତ-ଦେହା,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">arikuḷa-śoṇita-carccita-dehā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your body smeared with the blood of the hosts of your foes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବିଶ୍ବଭୂମଣ୍ଡଳ-କୃତବର-ସ୍ନେହା,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">biśbabhūmaṇḍaḷa-kr̥tabara-snehā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>who have shown your noble love to the whole round of the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">jananī, jananī, jananī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, Mother, Mother,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bande utkaḷa jananī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you, Mother Utkala.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">କବିକୁଳମୌଳି ସୁନନ୍ଦନ-ବନ୍ଦ୍ୟା,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">kabikuḷamauḷi sunandana-bandyā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>worshipped by your good sons, crowns of the race of poets [?],</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଭୁବନ ବିଘୋଷିତ-କୀର୍ତ୍ତି ଅନିନ୍ଦ୍ୟା,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bhubana bighoṣita-kīrtti anindyā,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>your fame proclaimed through the world, beyond reproach,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଧନ୍ୟେ, ପୁଣ୍ୟେ, ଚିର ଶରଣ୍ୟେ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">dhanye, puṇye, cira śaraṇye,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O blessed one, O holy one, refuge for ever,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ, ଜନନୀ,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">jananī, jananī, jananī,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mother, Mother, Mother,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="or">ବନ୍ଦେ ଉତ୍କଳ ଜନନୀ ।</span><span class="sg-r" lang="or-Latn">bande utkaḷa jananī.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I bow to you, Mother Utkala.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Laxmikanta Mohapatra (1912). Text from <a href="https://or.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%AC%AC%E0%AC%A8%E0%AD%8D%E0%AC%A6%E0%AD%87_%E0%AC%89%E0%AC%A4%E0%AD%8D%E0%AC%95%E0%AC%B3_%E0%AC%9C%E0%AC%A8%E0%AC%A8%E0%AD%80" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ISO 15919).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (16)</summary><ul><li>Romanization: ISO 15919 applied to the Odia letters as written: the inherent vowel written, as Odia sounds it (jananī, śarīra); ଳ is ḷ (utkaḷa, tāḷa, nīḷa, kaḷā), distinct from ଲ l; ବ is b throughout (bande, bidhauta, biśba), as Odia writes one letter for b and v; ୟ is ẏ, ଯ y, and the subscript ya of a conjunct y (puṇya, dhanye); ଋ and its sign r̥; ଂ ṁ; doubled letters kept as written (pūrṇṇa, kīrtti, carccita). The source's hyphens are kept and the danda । is given as a full stop.</li><li>The song is in a highly Sanskritized Odia: most lines are long compounds describing the Mother, addressed as a goddess-like figure of the land (Utkala is an old name of Odisha). The compounds are unpacked in the translation in their own order where English allows.</li><li>line 1.1: bande, 'I bow to, I salute' (Sanskrit vande), as in Bankim's Vande Mataram; jananī, 'mother' (she who gives birth).</li><li>line 1.2: hāsa, 'smile, laughter'; bhāṣa, 'speech, language': 'whose smile is lovely, whose speech is lovely'.</li><li>line 2.1: paẏodhi, 'the sea' (literally 'holder of waters'); pūta, 'pure, holy'.</li><li>line 2.2: tāḷa, the palmyra palm; tamāḷa, a dark-leaved tree of Sanskrit poetry. 2.3: śīkara, fine spray; samīra, breeze; taṭinī, river.</li><li>line 3.1: rājita-aṅge, 'on limbs made splendid': the forests adorn the Mother's body. 3.2: bhūdhara, 'mountain' ('earth-holder'); 'sāje taraṅge', 'is arrayed in waves' (range behind range).</li><li>line 3.3: 'kaḷa kaḷa', the sound of many birds (or of running water); mukharita, 'made to resound'.</li><li>line 4.2: 'jñāna-bijñāna', knowledge and (practical or discerning) knowledge; 'learning' or 'science' are both possible for the second word. 'pradarśita-netra', 'whose eyes show forth' (or 'whose eyes are shown': the compound can be read either way).</li><li>line 4.3: ūṭaja, a hut of leaves, a hermitage (usually spelt uṭaja; written here with a long ū).</li><li>line 5.2: 'cāru-kaḷā-baḷi-śobhita-beśā', 'whose dress shines with rows (or the whole range) of fine arts'.</li><li>line 5.3: tīrtha, a sacred bathing-place or place of pilgrimage; caya, 'a heap, a multitude'.</li><li>line 6.1: 'sura-bara-darpita-gehā', 'whose home is proud of (or made proud by) the best of the gods'; which god is meant the line does not say.</li><li>line 6.3: 'biśba-bhūmaṇḍaḷa-kr̥ta-bara-snehā', 'by whom excellent love has been shown to the whole circle of the earth'; bara can also be read 'boon' ('who has made her love a boon to the world').</li><li>line 7.1: the sense is uncertain [?]: kabikuḷa-mauḷi, 'crown (or crest-jewel) of the race of poets', may describe the sons who honour her, a single great poet, or the Mother herself; sunandana, 'good son' (or 'one who delights'); bandyā, 'to be worshipped, worshipped'.</li><li>line 7.3: dhanye, puṇye, śaraṇye are vocatives: 'O blessed one, O holy one, O refuge'.</li></ul></details></section>
